Date: Wed, 17 Mar 93 18:33:24 GMT
From: shunwton@reading.ac.uk
Subject: reason

> One problem with this is, there is no rational, measurable,
> non-circular definition of "self-interest".

  There is no non-circular definition of anything. You can only define a word 
in terms of other words, which themselves require definition, and so on until 
you eventually get a circle; concepts always have to be understood in terms of
being part of a web of concepts; hence the expression "hermeneutic circle".

> The schoolmen of the Middle Ages may have claimed that their beliefs were
> based on reason, but they were hampered by a scarcity of empirical evidence.

  Their beliefs were based on reason, that was the whole problem; they thought
that reason was superior to empirical evidence in describing the physical 
world. Galileo's point was that it doesn't matter how satisfactory the reasons
are that heavier objects should fall faster than lighter ones, if it doesn't
happen that way then it doesn't happen that way. Science was therefore a 
revolt against the rationalistic excesses of medaevil scholasticism. Put that
in your pipe and smoke it.

