Date: Thu, 18 Mar 93 06:22 EST
From: mlshew@dixie.com (Mark Shewmaker)
Subject: META: Opportunity for Spontaneous Order (Long)

As has been pointed out, one of the main reasons the extropians list
was set up in the first place was to provide a "safe haven"
for anarchocapitalists.  I was not among the charter subscribers,
but I can surely understand how at the time such like-minded people
would have wanted to be able to discuss interesting topics amongst
themselves without having to put up with the problems associated
with such attempts on the net, such as explaining the same things
over and over, countering hit-and-run argumentative posts, *again*
explaining things to newcomers, and just in general getting off track
of what you wanted to say by having to spend your time on side
discussions.  Discussing things with the greater net population
are fine and good, enjoyable and often illuminating even, but
if you can't get away from it all to just talk things over with
your friends, well, I can see how it can get annoying.

So what can a body do?  Well, if that body is hooked up to an
extropian mind through whatever interface is the current state of the
art, and that (and other) minds are already familiar with the net and
mailing lists, it can set up a "private" extropian mailing list.

Why should the mailing list be private?  To help prevent the kind of
mess you're escaping from on the net.  Should anything else be
different?  Well, you're all anarchocapitalists, right?  And you want
to talk about anarcho-capitalist and extropian stuff, right?  And you
really believe all that stuff about how your favored systems are
really the best, right?  Well, then:  Set up your mailing list
to reflect all that and have at it.

Thus, we have a mailing list which is operated on extropian principles,
or at least the administrators of the list set things up and operate
more closely to extropian principles than those on other mailing lists.
What's the result?  Well, it's a pretty good list, or at least it seems
that way to me.  I've been reading it since, oh, about October,
I believe, (except for a few weeks after Christmas when switching
accounts, and I missed it every day.)  The topics and messages are
usually very interesting and thought-provoking, there are a lot of
neat people here, a number of them even post regularly; all in all
it's a great list!

However, as some have recently noted, there has come to be a bit more
than strictly comfortable level of bickering and in-fighting; there
are definitely a lot of posts of questionable relation to extropian
topics, and there's a lot of wasted bandwidth.  What's going on here?
The things we wanted to escape from on the net are following us here!

Perhaps it would help to further identify the problem.  Greatly
exaggerated, the symptoms are these:  People are indiscriminately
posting to extropians, with callous disregard as to whether the
messages are of extropian interest.  Often these messages are way
off-topic, or are undistilled repetitions of nonsense, (forcing
the reader to unproductively spend time deciphering what turn out
to be a bunch of unredeeming garbage, but having vainly searched
for non-existent gems of information.)

We have a situation where individuals can take advantage of the
resources of the extropians mailing list, while destroying the
utility of the resource to the rest of the community.

Aha!

What we have here is a classic case of the tragedy of the commons.
We have a communal resource of which all can take part, but
no real system of resource allocation.  There are no price signals
flitting about.  That's what we need:  Not a communal system of
doomed-to-failure communal resources, but a capitalist system
of resource allocation providing the much-needed price signals.

So how do we go about getting them?  How can we orchestrate
a grand system simulating market activity on an electronic medium,
complete with full-blown reputation systems and centralized standards
to be used on the computers of everyone reading this mailing list,
with implementations available at little or no cost to the users,
instantly tying them in to this grand, unified, and very orderly,
simulated-allocation system that will blaze it's way into ISO
and beyond?

I don't know, and I'm not sure I'd want to be a part of it.  Not
being a huge fan of central economic control by the government, I'm
not to keen on similar ideas even by extropians.  I'd be skeptical
of the practicality, at least initially, of even a centralized
reputation scheme, attempting to enforce order from above.

But a distributed economic system similar to what occurs in the
real world is a different story, even if it still has to be
"simulated" somehow.

But we're still left with the question of how?

We know that things missing in our system now are those incredibly
useful price signals themselves.  We can't price things without a
currency, so we need is a currency.

(As an aside, we can try to make do with elaborate systems of
bartering virtual death-threats with each other, which on this list
would probably soon evolve into an undebasable monetary system
in it's own right.  In any event, a monetary system to begin with
would vastly simplify things.)

Let me give an example of what could happen if we had a currency,
and it was used by the mailing software.  (Note that the system
I propose is from one point of view entirely voluntary.
More on that later.)  For the sake of inaccuracy, I'll represent
the currency with the symbol "$".

For simplicity of discussion, I will make some assumptions:
Everyone gets extropians batch mode, so I can demonstrate doing
something with one batch of extropian letters.  Other assumptions
will be given in the text.

Suppose Bob wants to post a message.  Thinking it to be
a really interesting message, when ready to mail it, he inserts
into the header information a header line of:  "Exi-Funds: $2200",
and mails it to extropians.

All the extropians list management software does when it sees
that line is take that number, divide it by the number
of subscribed extropians, and re-send it to the subscribers.
Let's assume there are 200 extropians. Then I, a subscriber,
would get a message in my batch of messages with a header line
of "Exi-Funds: $11", like so:

     Date: Mon, 25 May 93 18:52:16 EST
     From: bob@site.com
     Subject: Review: Genetic Programming for Fun and Profit
     Exi-Funds: $11
     Exi-Message-ID: 3528 ($15000)

So, as read the messages, I have beside me a list of Message-IDs
such as the following:

     3526:
     3527:
     3528:
     3529:
     3530:

As I read each message, I rank it on on a scale from 0 to 100.  As it
happens, genetic programming is something I've really become
interested in as of late, although I've yet to read much about it
or even find out where it's mailing list is, :-), so I think that
3528 is really a great message.  It is more interesting that any
other message to me, so I rank it accordingly:

     3526: 30
     3528: 93
     3529: 3
     3530: 32

I didn't rank message 2537, so it gets a 50, as would any individual
omision, or even a group omission of the entire list--thus,
a voluntary system.

I then mail that list to extropian-funds@gnu.ai.mit.edu.

The list processing software then looks at the funds bid for each
message, reshuffles them in accordance with my preferences, and
sends them back to the posters.  (Or rather, credits their accounts
with the funds.)

There are different ways mathematically to do the reshuffling, things
which the user really shouldn't have to worry about all that much;
he'd just send in his rankings from 0 to 100 and let the mailing list
software handle the rest.

However, I'd imagine many of the readers here would be concerned about
such things, so I'll go into them.  Those of you who are not interested
in the math might want to skip to the table, and then a bit more.
(Search for "So much for the math, let's".)

The relevant variables for each message are the bid, ranking, and
cash-back:  b, r and c.  To clarify, these variable are the
per-person bid that I would see, ($11 in this case), the ranking
I give, (93 in this case), and the cash the poster gets back.
(Unknown at this point.)

I will assume the cost/paying distribution will be done in batches.
Perhaps within every extropian batch mailing, perhaps every 5 days,
perhaps every 100 messages.  That's not really important for my example,
so I will just for simplicity assume that it is done for each
batch mailing.

Now, for this batch of messages, we must be able to figure out the
cash-back.  For each message "n" We must find the cash-back as a
function of the set of bids and rankings:

 c( n ) = f( b1, b2, b3, ..., r1, r2, r3, ...)

But we want a constant money supply; that is, we want the sum of the
bids coming in to equal the cash-backs going out, or else there is no
monetary stability.

 SUM( c(n) ) = SUM ( b(n) )    (Equation 1)
  n             n

Within each batch, it would be nice if the cash-back were proportional
to the bid, and also proportional to your ranking of the message.
I will call the constant of proportionality "A":

 c(n) = A * b(n) * r(n)    (Equation 2)

We must solve for A, so put Equation 2 in the "SUM( c(n) )"
into Equation 1:                                n

 SUM( A * b(n) * r(n) ) = SUM( b's)
  n                        n

A is constant across the batch, so it can be pulled out of the summation:

 A * SUM( b(n) * r(n) ) = SUM( b(n) )
      n                    n

 A= SUM( b(n)) / SUM( b(n) * r(n) )
     n            n

So for every message "n", c=Abr.  In longhand, that's:

                           SUM( b(n))
 c(n) = b(n) * r(n) * --------------------
                       SUM( b(n) * r(n) )

Again, one nice thing about this equation is that within the messages
over which it is applied, the cash-back is proportional to both the
bid and the ranking.  Also, the cash-back is exactly equal to the bids
both when all the bids are the same, and also when all the rankings
are the same.

Here's a numerical example:

         Poster's       Message     Message   Bid times   Poster's
       Per-Person Bid   Number      Ranking    Ranking   Cash Back
            (b)          (n)         (r)        (br)        (c)

            03          3526:        30         90        1.883
            05          3525:        50        250        5.230
----->      11          3528:        93       1023       21.402
            03          3529:         3          9        0.188
            18          3530:        30        540       11.297
          -----------------------------------------------------
Totals:     40           n/a        206       1912       40.000
          SUM(B)                  SUM(R)     SUM(BR)     SUM(C)

Let's take the case of the favored message number 3528:  $11
of Bob's bid is under my control, and that $11 is included
the full $40 that is under my control for this batch.

I ranked his message with a ranking of 93.

So b = 11 and r = 93, and in fact, b*r is conveniently listed
in the table under br as 1023.

To solve c = Abr, we must find A, thus:

A = SUM(B) / SUM(BR), A = 40 / 1912 = 0.0209205

So, c = Abr = (0.0209205) * 1023 = 21.402

To verify parts of the design criteria:

1.  Messages 3526 and 3529 had the same bid, $3.  As predicted,
their payoff from me is proportional to their ranking by me,
that is:

 c(3526) / r(3526) = c(3529) / r(3529)
 1.883   / 30      = 0.188   / 3
             0.063 = 0.063

2.  Messages 33526 and 3530 had the same ranking, 30.  As predicted,
their payoff from me is proportional to their bids, that is:

 c(3526) / b(3526) = c(3530) / b(3530)
 1.883   / 3       = 11.297  / 18
             0.628 = 0.628

3.  The sum of the bids does equal the sum of the cash-backs:  $40.

So much for the math, let's look at the practical effects:  Bob
posted a message that was interesting to me; in effect he participated
in a distributed bet with a bunch of other people.  He thought that
more people would buy into his posting than other postings, and was
sure of it to the tune of $11.  (Actually, to the tune of $2200,
since the $11 was merely my share of it.)

If people pretty much did like his postings, then he'd get his money
back and then some.  If they didn't, then he wouldn't get so much
of his money back.  (Only in the case of everyone giving him a rating
of 0 would he not get any money back at all.)

In any event, he won money in the deal from me, whether he made
money on the whole is still an open question.

In this way, everyone's postings start competing in a capitalist
marketplace!

By the way, the money bid in the individual posts are never at any
point in the reader's accounts--it's never the reader's money, but
can only be transferred between poster's accounts.  Think on this
for a while to see why you wouldn't want it the other way.

We would, of course, need a few more things to have it work *really*
well.

One very important thing is the ability to transfer funds between
people directly, so we'd need some sort of system to allow that.
It could even be incorporated into extropian-funds@gnu.ai.mit.edu.

Now the possibilities are tremendous!

o  Do you really, really like the posts of someone?

   Donate him some money.

o  Do you have a complaint with another person and for some reason
   don't want to go through the official list procedures?

   Do you also really like the idea of PPLs?

   Then contract with a third party to settle the dispute.

o  Want more funds, but aren't good at writing messages?

  Offer a list-PPL service, or offer futures contracts based on
  the projected worth of other members,

  Or bet that Perry will be convinced of Flying Yogis within
  the next year, or whatever.

But wait, that's not all!  It's not even the half of it!

o I see possibilities for a second list, which could be sent
  computer-readable control messages, to allow people to build,
  supply, and sell their own services to be transacted through
  this second list.

  o If somebody wants to read flying yogi posts and
    only flying yogi posts, perhaps he can contract out with someone
    else to supply him with lists of Exi-Message-IDs that will
    identify those posts.

  o If a lot of people directed their ranking lists there,

    o Individual subscribers could use that info to have
      their own programs reading it develop decentralized
      webs of reputation.

    o Exi get's competition.

o There is question of privacy, but:

  o I would hope for general debugging and fine-tuning reasons
    that the posts would be public anyway for the first few weeks
    at least, then maybe by request afterwards.  (That is, if
    there is a second list for this purpose.  I do not want control
    posts cluttering up the main list.)

  o They could be anonymized by the software, although with the
    popular posters, there's not really much security there.

  o The cypherpunks among us can always start taking advantage
    of encryption.

But back to the world of just a ranking system and funds transfer:

o  It would be best if each post showed the poster's account
   balance.  That way, everyone else could see if their posts were
   interesting to other people, and vice-versa.  I would hope that
   information was always printed.  (This is something to think about.)

o  For the initial distribution of funds, I would suggest equal funds
   being given to all subscribers, with the exception that Extropy
   subscribers, Exi members, and Exi Board members get a progressively
   larger initial share.  Or really, however Harry wants to do it.
   When you get down to it, the initial distribution really isn't
   that important.

o  There should be a minimum bid level, and a default bid level.
   I don't now how to suggest what the minimum should be, other
   that it should not be less than zero.  The default could be
   the average bid of the past two weeks.  People who don't want
   to bother with the system are still tracked, and still have
   their balances updated with the bids, and can by their account
   balance still see whether they are being enlightening or just
   a general pain to everyone, (and everyone else can see
   the balance, too), but they don't have to bother with setting up
   the bids, or ranking other people if they don't want to.

   If you have a problem with "imposing" this system on someone
   who doesn't care to participate, consider the parallels with
   a moderated list/newsgroup.

   In any event, it's still pretty much transparent to the people
   who aren't interested so much.  They just have to put up with
   two more header lines, and the knowledge that if they make
   a fool of themselves and continually post useless messages,
   that their unpopularity will show.

o  There's no real problem if an individual's account balance goes
   negative.  So what?  The real use of this system lies in the
   fact that you can measure things and see responses to your articles.
   A negative balance either means that people really don't like
   your articles, or that they like them, but you are simply posting
   amongst geniuses, or that you are a newcomer.  After all, you don't
   really purchase a futures contract for the purpose of buying the
   oil itself.

o  A noninflating currency implies that newcomers to the list get no
   newly-printed new currency:  They start out at zero.  This isn't
   such a big deal as you might at first think.  I really think it'd
   be the way to go.  People leaving the list should either arrange
   to give someone else their money, or have it held by someone else
   until they get back, or risk losing it by default to Exi.

   (There'd have to be a way to deal with the case of people who
   leave with a negative balance, though.)

o  I've thought quite a bit on various taxation schemes, both with
   Exi automatically charging a percentage to process the articles,
   and the same for the individual users to read the articles,
   the former as an attempt to keep Exi in control of discussion,
   and the latter an attempt to "pay" readers for their time, and
   give newcoming lurkers a chance to get funds to post with a
   positive bank balance left.

   I've come to think of the latter idea as downright silly and
   counter-productive, and the former idea as, well, not as useful
   as you might at first think, while setting bad precedent, and
   sort of being un-extropian.  In any event, such so-called taxation
   is a topic to itself.

o  This is a chance to do the "other half" of cypherpunks.  There's no
   need at this point for us to require secure digital postage stamps
   on our articles or any such stuff like that yet--the cypherpunks
   are exploring all that.  What extropians can provide, however,
   is a test-market showing in advance what people will
   demand/use/provide/pay for to utilize those systems.

o  One neat thing is that there's not really *that* much that needs to
   be changed to the list software to do the basic bidding system I
   proposed.  Now who will bell the cat?  :-)

   What is needed, though:

    o A program to handle accounting and move money between
      accounts at the request of a mail server or other exi
      programs.  (Just to be clear, I *do not* propose cypher
      money, just insecure listings of account balances
      in a central computer.  It's a lot easier, and the
      centralization doesn't really matter that much
      for our purposes.)  The program should never loose
      track of money due to, say, executing floating point
      operations.

    o An initial distribution of play money.  (For ease of expansion
      and other reasons, I suggest large amounts of money, since
      the total quantity of money doesn't really matter as long
      as it's constant.)

    o The mail server itself to allow subscribers to transfer
      the play money from their own account to another account.

    o Modifications to the list processing software to:

      o Find the exi-funds in the header incoming messages,
        divide it by the number of subscribers, and re-send it
        in the header of all messages.

      o But if the number was below the minimum, or nonexistent,
        insert the minumum number instead.

      o Put the account balance of the poster in the outgoing header.

      o Generate a unique exi-id for each message and put it in the
        header.

      o Recognize and accept ranked lists of id's.

      o Process those lists every (Batched message/n batched message/
        /n days/n messages/???)

      o Generate empty accounts for newcomers, and do something
        with the funds of exiting members who make not plans for
        the funds.  (Keep it all? Distribute it proportionally among
        existing members? Put it in an incoming members trust fund?)

      o Handle negative accounts of outgoing members appropriately,
        too.  (Keep a prioritized list of not-even-real-play money
        to be extinguished as needed to account for generated money
        from negative balances?)

      o Inform members of account balances regularly in some way.
        (In batch message headers?  Weekly statement lists?)

      o Consider future expansion of more detailed account
        information.  (This will quite possibly happen of
        it's own accord on an extropians-funds list.)

o  It might be possible to further modify the code to add a cost
   for using or not using (popular/unpopular) prefixes, said cost
   to be determined by the market, of course.

   Maybe by then people will realize that "prefix" is just another
   way of saying "newsgroup"  1/2 :-)

o  What if it becomes really successful?  What if new-agers
   then start to think of this list as a safe haven and vote
   their messages popular?

   Let them have their own newsgroup, er, prefix, and don't let
   it bother you; rather, let them benefit from a good healthy
   dose of capitalism.

o  Hopefully, the above problem won't happen before extropians
   becomes like a mini-usenet.

o  If it does become like a mini usenet in the future, eventually
   someone will put the both halves of the equation together, putting
   together some cypherpunk stuff and various utilities with a more
   distributed version of what I've discussed here, and start adding
   it to the net itself.

o  That would mean that imply competing currencies could develop
   on the net, which would be pretty cool.

o  It would also mean that extropians could go back to usenet, but
   in something like a moderated group--either only posting things bid
   with extropian money, or maybe just with some of the users only
   reading extropian-funded postings.

Walking closer to the real world once again:

o  One of the neatest things about all this is that it requires
   little "imposed order", but instead sets things up for
   spontaneous order to develop.

o  I mentioned the Bell-the-Cat analogy earlier.  I realize it's
   very relevant.

o  One thing I skimmed over in my example is in making a default
   ranking of 50.  Note that if you have one 50, and the rest 90's,
   the 50 will loose money to the 90's.  Don't mistake a ranking of
   50 with a ranking such that the cash-back equals the bid.

   I thought of suggesting that as the default, but

    o It doesn't really get you anything.  If you want default
      response of the user neglecting to list an ID to make a
      no-effect ranking, don't bother, just have the
      list-processing software pay back the bid exactly,
      it's much less work.

    o A default value of 50 ensures standardization of the
      measurements of mediocrity, and preserves the fact that it
      shifts in importance and value in the face of other posts.

      ie, a mediocre post that occurs on a day with a lot of
      terrible posts will likely be rewarded, whereas if it occurs
      on a day with a lot of genius posts, it will loose money.
      In my opinion this is a good thing.

    o Not that it's directly related to the utility of the idea, but
      The equations for determining the equilibrium ranking are not
      easily renderable in ascii form.  I figured out the one for the
      case of one ranking to be found, which was bad enough in itself,
      Multiple rankings quickly get more complicated, In any event,
      it would also load the server more for no good reason.

o  Have fun.

