> 	1) quits talking about them on the list

In general, I *haven't* talked about them on the list --- before the
challenge, that is.  I'd occasionally react to someone else's flailing
about on the topic, but I was always careful to keep it short and on point.

> 	2) seriously considers that maybe Tim is right

Neopaganism (my skeptical, hard-headed brand of it, anyhow) has been
working for me for fifteen years now.  Tim's thumbs-down would have
to be pretty damn persuasive to neutralize that.

> 	3) stays on the list to hear what non-pagans think

You clearly don't understand why I framed the bet as I did.  Tim does,
so I won't belabor the point.

> 	4) read "Retreat to Commitment" by Bartley

Maybe I'll do that anyhow if it's in print.  ISBN?  Publisher?  One-paragraph
synopsis?
 
> Leaving the list if the ideas are judged as non-rational means that
> the ideas are not the thing being challenged.

Oh?  What, then, do you think Tim and I have been discussing?  Chopped
liver?

>                                              I know that some people
> have already leaned on Tim to fudge the judgement so that Eric can
> remain on the list.

That would be wrong and dishonorable and I trust Tim not to do it.  Not
that I think he'll want or need to.

Remember the terms of the challenge.  I've asked Tim to judge whether
neopaganism is "compatible with a rational mind-set".  That's almost
too easy; for the weakest possible thumbs-up all he'd have to do is exhibit
one person who is both a neopagan and has a fundamentally rational mind-set.

This is not what I'm after, though, and Tim's made it clear to me that he
understands that.  It's the lowest end of a spectrum of positive responses
ranging through:

	1) Some neopagans are rational people, but that's in spite of their
	   belief system, not because of it.

	2) Neopagan belief is basically *orthogonal* to philosophical
	   rationality (there are a number of reasons this might be
	   true).

	3) Neopaganism actively *promotes* rationality.

My own position is somewhere between 2 and 3; I don't claim that neopaganism
intrinsically cultivates more respect for, say, the scientific method --- but
I do think it's a benign occupier of the memetic receptor sites on which
religious irrationality would otherwise flourish.
