From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: THEOLOGY: (was The Internet Witch Trial)

I know that a lot of people have complained about this thread, and I have
thus been trying to stay away from it.  But I really do have something to
add that is new and Extropian.  Honest!

Eric puts forward some views on the relationship between paganism:

>	2) Neopagan belief is basically *orthogonal* to philosophical
>	   rationality (there are a number of reasons this might be
>	   true).
>
>	3) Neopaganism actively *promotes* rationality.
>
>My own position is somewhere between 2 and 3; I don't claim that neopaganism
>intrinsically cultivates more respect for, say, the scientific method --- but
>I do think it's a benign occupier of the memetic receptor sites on which
>religious irrationality would otherwise flourish.

That is a very clear and succinct defense of paganism: It is memetic
innoculation.  But perhaps there is a duty to find the vaccine that will
most closely jibe with rationality.  Extropians will, in any case, want to
find the memetic defense that most fully supports their world view.

I sometimes wonder if Extropianism would not serve as a better defense
against religious irrationality if it made more explicit the theological
ramifications of transhumanism.  What sort of theological tenets are
compatible with Extropianism?  Very briefly, I've come up with three views:

1) God is the sysop of the system in which we are now simulated;
2) God(s) exist(s) now, and it/they is/are our future selves, who have
grown so powerful as to transcend the barriers of time;
3) Gods do not yet exist, but we can now believe that we will become
one/them.

Hans Moravec takes something like the first view.  Dave Ross does, too, but
am not sure whether he maintains that the Sysop is our future selves or
another being altogether.  Viewing the Sysop as our future selves is surely
more conducive to Extropians, and no less ludicrous.  

"A" took the view in 2), in its EXTROPY article titled "Arch-Anarchy."  But
A tended toward a unitary view of Godhood.  A view more compatible with
Extropianism would allow for multiple gods, I think.

Some version of 3) is often espoused by Extropians, though they seem to
care little about the current theological ramifications about this view.

Why should an Extropian care about these reliberous theologies?  Because,
if coupled with appropriate ceremonies, they could serve as very defenses
against religious memes.  They could also postively advance Extropianism
insofar as they encourage us to envision our future potential selves and
try to achieve our ideals.  

Let me share a personal example with you:  I have been meditating twice
daily for well over six years now.  It's nothing fancy--just good posture,
paced breathing, and visualization.  I always start and end by picturing my
ideal self In between I think about how to achieve my ideal self.  (I also
visualize nice things about my friends and family, and always include a
random "guest" to keep my empathy muscles in tone).  Now, it has occurred
to me that this looks a lot like a theological practice that accepts the
view put forth in 3), above.  Yet this exercise does not involve mindless
worship or blind faith.  And, certainly, I have managed to keep free from
religious memes.

There are, of course, dangers to Extropian theology.  But I'll let the rest
of you bring those up.

Anyhow, the point is that Eric's defense of paganism may be incomplete. 
Rationality may demand that he use not just any memetic vaccine, but the
one that is most removed from mysticism and most compatible with practical
and empirical procedures.  I'm suggesting that an properly developed
Extropian theology might qualify as such a replacement.  Perhaps Tim should
excuse Eric for buying into already existing memetic vaccines.  But as for
myself, I'm going to custom craft one that will both feed my memetic hunger
and boost my Extropian goals.

